TITLE:: BlipSync
SUMMARY:: Band-limited harmonic-band oscillator with phase I/O and sub-sample hard sync
CATEGORIES:: UGens>Generators>Deterministic
RELATED:: Classes/Blip, Classes/Saw, Classes/Pulse, Classes/Phasor

DESCRIPTION::

A band-limited impulse train in the spirit of link::Classes/Blip::, rebuilt around
four changes:

LIST::
## The band is specified in STRONG::Hz::, not as a harmonic count, so the spectral
width does not follow the fundamental around.
## The band edges are STRONG::fractional::. A harmonic fades in or out linearly
instead of switching on or off, so sweeping any parameter produces no stepping,
no crossfade transient and no zipper noise.
## Every input is read STRONG::per sample::, so audio-rate FM and PM behave
correctly. Blip samples its frequency input once per control block.
## Two timbral controls that keep the alias-free guarantee: a STRONG::phase
rotation:: that makes the impulse asymmetric without touching the magnitude
spectrum, and an exponential STRONG::spectral tilt:: that softens it.
## Phase is both an STRONG::input and an output::, and there is a sub-sample
accurate hard sync input with polyBLEP step correction.
::

BlipSync returns TWO channels: code::[ waveform, phase ]::. The phase output is a
0..1 ramp in cycles, intended for coupling, sync chains and analysis -- it is a
raw sawtooth and is not band-limited, so do not listen to it directly.

STRONG::How it works::

The output is the closed form of a weighted cosine sum

CODE::
y(p) = (1/W) * sum_k  w_k * cos(2*pi*k*p)
w_k  = clip(maxfreq/freq - k + 1, 0, 1) * clip(k - minfreq/freq + 1, 0, 1)
::

evaluated as a shifted Dirichlet kernel. The 0/0 singularity that sits exactly on
the impulse peak is removed analytically rather than guarded against, so there is
no reciprocal-sine table and no interpolation error: the waveform is accurate to
about -218 dB, against about -51 dB for Blip.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: ar

ARGUMENT:: freq
Fundamental frequency in Hz. Audio or control rate. Negative values run the
oscillator backwards. Default 440.

ARGUMENT:: maxfreq
Top of the harmonic band in Hz -- the band limit. Harmonics are included up to
this frequency, with the topmost one faded in fractionally. Internally clamped to
just under Nyquist; when STRONG::freq:: itself rises past the limit the
oscillator fades to silence instead of folding over. Default 20000.

ARGUMENT:: minfreq
Bottom of the harmonic band in Hz. 0 (the default) means the band starts at the
fundamental, i.e. an ordinary impulse train. Raising it turns the oscillator into
a band of harmonics -- a formant-like burst whose centre and width you dial in Hz,
independently of pitch.

ARGUMENT:: phase
Phase offset in CYCLES (0..1), NOT radians. Added to the running phase rather
than accumulated into it, so an audio-rate signal here is phase modulation. Note
that the phase OUTPUT includes this offset. Default 0.

ARGUMENT:: sync
Sync input. See STRONG::syncMode::. Default 0.

ARGUMENT:: syncPhase
Phase, in cycles, to jump to when sync fires. Nonzero values give the classic
offset hard-sync timbres. Default 0.

ARGUMENT:: syncMode
INIT RATE. code::0:: -- STRONG::sync:: is a trigger and fires on a rising
crossing of zero; the crossing time is interpolated from the two samples either
side. code::1:: -- STRONG::sync:: is a 0..1 phase ramp (another BlipSync's phase
output, link::Classes/Phasor::, code::LFSaw.ar(f).range(0,1)::) and fires on the
wrap. Mode 1 recovers the crossing time exactly and is the accurate way to chain
oscillators. Default 0.

ARGUMENT:: iphase
INIT RATE. Initial phase in cycles. Use it to spread the starting phases of an
oscillator bank. Default 0.

ARGUMENT:: normalize
INIT RATE. code::0:: -- peak normalisation, the waveform peaks at 1.0 like Blip;
narrow bands are therefore quiet. code::1:: -- RMS normalisation, roughly
constant loudness as the band width changes, which is usually what you want when
sweeping STRONG::maxfreq::. Default 0.

ARGUMENT:: rotate
Constant phase rotation applied to every harmonic, in CYCLES. Not the same as
STRONG::phase::, which rotates harmonic k by k times the angle and is therefore
only a time shift; this rotates them all by the SAME angle, which makes the
impulse asymmetric.

LIST::
## code::0:: -- symmetric impulse (identical to the UGen before this existed)
## code::0.125:: -- leaning
## code::0.25:: -- the Hilbert transform of the impulse: sharp edge one side,
slow tail the other
## code::0.5:: -- inverted impulse
::

The magnitude spectrum is unchanged, so this cannot alias and is safe at audio
rate -- unlike phase modulation, the modulation index does not scale with the
harmonic number. RMS is exactly invariant too: rotating changes the crest factor,
not the loudness. In peak-normalised mode the waveform peak falls to about 0.73
at code::0.25::, which is headroom rather than a level drop.

Most audible on impulsive material -- low fundamentals where every period reads
as a separate event. Much less so on steady tones at high fundamentals. Default 0.

ARGUMENT:: tilt
Exponential spectral tilt in dB per kHz, referenced to the fundamental. 0 (the
default) is the flat, brick-wall band; negative values roll the harmonics off
and give a smooth, ring-free pulse rather than a sinc-like one. Positive values
tilt upward.

It is a straight line in dB against LINEAR frequency (the harmonic weights form
a geometric series, which is what keeps the closed form), and it applies
UNDERNEATH the STRONG::maxfreq:: brick wall, so the output stays alias-free at
any tilt. Like everything else here it is specified in Hz terms, so the slope
stays put when the pitch moves.

Useful range is roughly 0 to -12 dB/kHz. Worth reaching for when there is
saturation downstream: a brick-wall impulse train is very peaky, so a waveshaper
mostly flattens its tip; a tilted pulse is wider and distorts far more evenly.

ARGUMENT:: track
INIT RATE. 0 (the default) derives the band width from STRONG::freq:: alone.
1 derives it from the TOTAL phase velocity, code::freq + d(phase)/dt::.

Two uses.

STRONG::Drive the oscillator entirely from phase.:: Set code::freq: 0:: and feed
STRONG::phase:: a code::Phasor::, another BlipSync's phase output, or anything
else that ramps; the band limit then follows whatever rate that source is
running at. Without tracking this is silent, because code::maxfreq/0:: is zero
harmonics. Measured against the equivalent freq-driven oscillator (200 Hz,
code::maxfreq: 8000::), the 40 harmonic amplitudes agree to 0.0035 dB and the
worst spurious component above 10 kHz is -142 dBFS.

STRONG::Band-limit phase modulation.:: With tracking on, a fast modulator on
STRONG::phase:: narrows the band as the instantaneous frequency rises instead of
folding over. For a 300 Hz carrier at code::maxfreq: 6000:: with +-942 Hz of
deviation, aliasing measures -43 dB relative to signal with tracking against
-21 dB without. The timbre changes as it does so -- this is dynamic band
limiting, not a transparent fix.

The velocity estimate is smoothed with a 0.25 ms one-pole, because differencing
a float32 phase signal turns its quantisation into high-frequency noise on the
band edge. So the band lags a genuinely abrupt change of drive rate by that
much, and a phase input that stops moving asks for an infinitely narrow impulse
and gets near-silence.

METHOD:: arSig
As code::ar::, but returns only the waveform channel and accepts
code::mul:: / code::add::.

EXAMPLES::

CODE::
s.boot;

// 1. the basic thing: bandwidth in Hz, so timbre stays put across the keyboard
(
{
    var f = MouseX.kr(50, 800, 1);
    BlipSync.ar(f, 4000).at(0) * 0.2 ! 2
}.play
)

// compare with Blip, where a fixed harmonic count means the brightness
// tracks the pitch and the harmonics audibly step in and out
(
{
    var f = MouseX.kr(50, 800, 1);
    Blip.ar(f, 20) * 0.2 ! 2
}.play
)

// 2. sweeping the band limit: no stepping, no zipper
(
{
    var top = SinOsc.kr(0.2).exprange(300, 16000);
    BlipSync.ar(110, top, normalize: 1).at(0) * 0.2 ! 2
}.play
)

// 3. minfreq turns it into a moving band of harmonics (formant-ish)
(
{
    var centre = SinOsc.kr(0.13).exprange(300, 5000);
    var width  = 800;
    BlipSync.ar(80, centre + width, (centre - width).max(0), normalize: 1).at(0) * 0.2 ! 2
}.play
)

// 4. audio-rate FM actually works here
(
{
    var mod = SinOsc.ar(XLine.kr(1, 800, 8)) * 200;
    BlipSync.ar(220 + mod, 8000).at(0) * 0.2 ! 2
}.play
)

// 5. hard sync from a phase-ramp master (syncMode 1 -- sample-exact)
(
{
    var master = Phasor.ar(0, 110 * SampleDur.ir, 0, 1);
    var slave  = BlipSync.ar(
        freq: MouseX.kr(110, 2200, 1),
        maxfreq: 8000,
        sync: master,
        syncMode: 1
    );
    slave[0] * 0.2 ! 2
}.play
)

// 6. phase output -> Kuramoto-style coupling between two oscillators.
// Each oscillator pulls its frequency toward the other's phase.
(
{
    var k = MouseY.kr(0, 60);
    var fb = LocalIn.ar(2);                 // the two phase outputs, 0..1
    var d  = sin(2pi * (fb[1] - fb[0]));    // phase difference
    var a  = BlipSync.ar(200 + (k * d),      6000, iphase: 0.0);
    var b  = BlipSync.ar(203 - (k * d),      6000, iphase: 0.37);
    LocalOut.ar([a[1], b[1]]);
    (a[0] + b[0]) * 0.15 ! 2
}.play
)

// 6b. a full Kuramoto bank of n oscillators, all-to-all.
//     The pairwise sum (1/n)*sum_j sin(2pi(th_j - th_i)) is exactly
//     S*cos(2pi th_i) - C*sin(2pi th_i) with C, S the mean of cos and sin
//     over all phases -- so the mean field costs O(n) UGens, not O(n^2).
//     n=16: 229 UGens instead of 905, and the result is identical.
//     Raise k past the spread of the natural frequencies and the bank locks.
(
{
    var n = 8, k = MouseY.kr(0, 80);
    var base = Array.fill(n, { |i| 90 + (i * 4.3) });
    var ph = LocalIn.ar(n);
    var c = Mix(cos(2pi * ph)) / n, s = Mix(sin(2pi * ph)) / n;
    var osc = Array.fill(n, { |i|
        var field = (s * cos(2pi * ph[i])) - (c * sin(2pi * ph[i]));
        BlipSync.ar(base[i] + (k * field), 6000, tilt: -2)
    });
    LocalOut.ar(osc.collect(_.at(1)));
    Splay.ar(osc.collect(_.at(0))) * 0.15
}.play
)

// 6c. same bank, but pulled toward an external master phase as well
//     (this is the PLL form: correct towards a reference ramp)
(
{
    var n = 8, k = 30, m = MouseY.kr(0, 60);
    var base = Array.fill(n, { |i| 90 + (i * 4.3) });
    var master = Phasor.ar(0, 100 * SampleDur.ir, 0, 1);
    var ph = LocalIn.ar(n);
    var c = Mix(cos(2pi * ph)) / n, s = Mix(sin(2pi * ph)) / n;
    var osc = Array.fill(n, { |i|
        var field = (s * cos(2pi * ph[i])) - (c * sin(2pi * ph[i]));
        var lock  = sin(2pi * (master - ph[i]));
        BlipSync.ar(base[i] + (k * field) + (m * lock), 6000, tilt: -2)
    });
    LocalOut.ar(osc.collect(_.at(1)));
    Splay.ar(osc.collect(_.at(0))) * 0.15
}.play
)

// 6d. driven from an external phase bank instead: any UGen that emits 0..1
//     ramps (a PLL, a Phasor bank, phases read from a buffer) becomes the
//     clock, and track: 1 lets the band follow whatever rate it runs at
(
{
    var ph = Array.fill(4, { |i| Phasor.ar(0, (97 + (i * 5)) * SampleDur.ir, 0, 1) });
    Splay.ar(ph.collect { |p| BlipSync.ar(0, 6000, phase: p, track: 1, tilt: -2).at(0) }) * 0.2
}.play
)

// 7. phase offset as an audio-rate input = phase modulation
(
{
    var pm = SinOsc.ar(220) * MouseX.kr(0, 1);
    BlipSync.ar(220, 6000, phase: pm).at(0) * 0.2 ! 2
}.play
)

// 8. rotate: asymmetric impulses. Sweep it and listen to the attack flip.
(
{
    var r = MouseX.kr(0, 1);
    BlipSync.ar(60, 6000, rotate: r).at(0) * 0.2 ! 2
}.play
)

// 9. rotate through a waveshaper -- the crest factor change is the point
(
{
    var r = SinOsc.kr(0.1).range(0, 0.5);
    var sig = BlipSync.ar(40, 9000, rotate: r).at(0);
    (RLPF.ar(sig, 1200, 0.3) * 20).tanh * 0.15 ! 2
}.play
)

// 10. tilt: brick wall vs smooth rolloff, same band limit
(
{
    var t = MouseY.kr(0, -12);
    BlipSync.ar(80, 12000, tilt: t).at(0) * 0.2 ! 2
}.play
)

// 11. both, in a pulsar-ish texture
(
{
    var n = 24;
    var f = Array.exprand(n, 4, 40);
    var pulses = BlipSync.ar(f, Array.exprand(n, 4000, 16000),
        rotate: Array.rand(n, 0, 1), tilt: Array.rand(n, -6.0, 0.0)).collect(_.at(0));
    var filt = RLPF.ar(pulses, Array.exprand(n, 200, 6000), Array.rand(n, 0.02, 0.4));
    Splay.ar((filt * 12).tanh) * 0.3
}.play
)

// 12b. driven entirely from an external phase: freq 0, track 1.
//      the band limit follows the Phasor's rate, so it stays alias-free
//      while you scrub, warp or stop the ramp
(
{
    var rate = MouseX.kr(20, 600, 1);
    var ph = Phasor.ar(0, rate * SampleDur.ir, 0, 1);
    BlipSync.ar(0, 6000, phase: ph, track: 1).at(0) * 0.2 ! 2
}.play
)

// 12c. one clock, several timbres: every voice reads the same phase ramp, so
//      they are locked to the sample and only the band and offset differ
(
{
    var ph = Phasor.ar(0, 60 * SampleDur.ir, 0, 1);
    Splay.ar(
        Array.fill(5, { |i|
            BlipSync.ar(0, 800 * (i + 1), phase: ph + (i / 40),
                        track: 1, tilt: -2).at(0)
        })
    ) * 0.15
}.play
)

// 12. an oscillator bank with spread initial phases
(
{
    var n = 8;
    Mix.fill(n, { |i|
        BlipSync.ar(100 * (i + 1), 12000, iphase: i / n).at(0)
    }) * 0.05 ! 2
}.play
)
::

STRONG::Notes::

LIST::
## Hard sync responds one sample after the detected edge: the crossing is found
between two samples, and the polyBLEP correction can only be scheduled forward.
There is no output latency.
## Hard sync is the only remaining aliasing source, and it is inherent -- a step
discontinuity has infinite bandwidth. The correction puts it around -28 dB
relative to the signal, against -19 dB for an uncorrected reset. Lowering
STRONG::maxfreq:: reduces it further.
## The fractional top edge sits one harmonic above STRONG::maxfreq::. If that
would cross Nyquist it is dropped, which reintroduces stepping at the very top.
Keep STRONG::maxfreq:: at least one harmonic spacing below Nyquist -- the 20000
default does so for any fundamental under about 4 kHz.
## A fundamental above the band limit fades out rather than folding over.
## The band limit applies to the UNMODULATED spectrum. Deep FM or PM spreads each
harmonic well past STRONG::maxfreq:: and will alias; lower STRONG::maxfreq:: when
modulating hard, or use code::track: 1:: to make PM band-limit itself.
## code::normalize: 1:: holds the RMS at 0.707 for any bandwidth, so the peak is
sqrt(W) -- about 7.4 for a 54-harmonic band. Scale accordingly.
## code::ar:: has no mul/add because it returns two channels and madd would scale
the phase output too. Use code::.at(0) * amp:: or link::#*arSig::.
## code::rotate:: is a pure phase rotation, so it cannot introduce aliasing or
change loudness at any modulation rate, and code::tilt:: does not relax the
STRONG::maxfreq:: band limit.
## CPU is about 0.09% of one core per voice with control-rate parameters, 0.15%
with rotate or tilt engaged, 0.21% when freq, minfreq, maxfreq or tilt is audio
rate -- independent of how many harmonics the band contains.
::
